NOTES.push.apply(NOTES,[{"s": 4, "topic": "Weather charts and forecasts (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "The Skyway Code explains the UK GA weather products. The F215 forecasts weather below 10,000 ft for a 9-hour period, the F214 gives upper winds and temperatures, and the GAMET is a plain-text low-level forecast issued four times a day for four UK regions. TAFs are forecasts and METARs are reports, with cloud heights above aerodrome level. A ceiling is BKN or OVC, and legal VFR minima are limits, not targets. Built from the Skyway Code pp.33-40.", "sections": [["F215, F214 and GAMET", ["<b>F215</b> forecasts in-flight weather below <code>10,000 ft</code> for the British Isles over a <code>9-hour</code> period centred on a fixed time. F415 is the near-Europe version", "The F215 shows fronts and pressure centres at the chart time, zones lettered A, B, C with cloud amount, type and heights above mean sea level, visibility, ice and turbulence, and the freezing level (0°C isotherm). <code>XXX</code> means tops above the chart limit", "<b>F214</b> gives forecast upper winds and temperatures at 'spot' positions (columns: altitude in 1,000s of feet, wind direction, speed, temperature). F414 is near Europe", "<b>GAMET</b> is an abbreviated plain-text low-level forecast issued four times a day for four UK regions. It replaced AIRMETs and includes the synoptic summary, hazards, freezing level and zone forecasts"]], ["Reading TAFs and METARs", ["TAFs are <b>forecasts</b>, METARs are <b>reports</b> of actual weather, normally updated every half hour", "Example: <code>34008KT</code> is wind from 340° at 8 kt. <code>9999</code> means visibility 10 km or more. <code>SCT025</code> is 3 to 4 oktas at 2,500 ft above aerodrome level (AAL)", "Cloud type is not reported unless it is cumulonimbus or towering cumulus", "<b>TEMPO</b> is a temporary change within a period. <b>PROB30</b> and <b>PROB40</b> are the only probabilities used in the UK. If PROB30/40 or uncertain timings appear, look at the overall situation more thoroughly", "Cloud is given above the <b>aerodrome</b>, so compare with terrain on your route. A 1,500 ft ceiling can hide hill tops", "Viewing reports on a map helps build the weather picture. A <b>SIGMET</b> warns of severe weather such as thunderstorms, mountain waves, icing or turbulence"]], ["Icing in the Skyway Code", ["If you enter visible moisture at <code>0°C</code> or below, airframe ice may form. The worst usually occurs between <code>0°C and -15°C</code>", "Always remove frost, ice and snow, and keep the pitot and static ports clear", "Carb ice comes from the pressure and temperature drop in the venturi. It often forms outside visible moisture with high humidity and is more likely at low power", "The carb icing risk chart plots dewpoint against air temperature and shows serious icing at any power or at descent power, so use carb heat per the AFM", "Fuel-injected engines can still ice at the air intake: use alternate air"]], ["Making the weather decision", ["Know the conditions linked with warm and cold fronts, high and low pressure and thunderstorms", "<b>Cloud ceiling</b> is the lowest cloud covering more than half the sky (BKN or OVC). <b>Cloud base</b> is the lowest visible cloud and includes FEW and SCT", "Legal VFR minima are <b>limits, not targets</b>. Class G can allow 1,500 m visibility clear of cloud but the cloud height often forces dangerously low flight", "Ceiling of <code>1,500 ft AGL</code> or less needs particular attention to terrain. Below <code>1,000 ft AGL</code> is normally for circuits or familiar local flying", "Surface visibility under <code>5 km</code> is not recommended for VFR: the horizon is unlikely to be clear. Haze towards the sun and low winter sun can reduce visibility", "Winds above about <code>35 kt</code> aloft often mean bumpy conditions, and strong headwinds affect fuel", "If you do not hold an IR or IMC rating, you must not enter cloud"]]], "cards": [["What period does an F215 cover?", "A 9-hour period centred on a fixed time, below 10,000 ft."], ["What does the F214 provide?", "Forecast upper winds and temperatures."], ["How often is a GAMET issued?", "Four times a day for four UK regions."], ["What does XXX on the F215 mean?", "Cloud tops above the chart upper limit."], ["What is the difference between a TAF and a METAR?", "A TAF is a forecast, a METAR is a report of the actual weather."], ["What does 9999 mean in visibility?", "10 km or more."], ["Cloud heights in TAFs and METARs are measured from where?", "Above aerodrome level."], ["What does TEMPO mean?", "A temporary condition within the stated period."], ["Which probabilities are used in UK TAFs?", "PROB30 and PROB40 only."], ["What is a SIGMET?", "A warning of severe weather such as thunderstorms, mountain waves, icing or turbulence."], ["Define cloud ceiling.", "The lowest cloud covering more than half the sky (BKN or OVC)."], ["Worst airframe icing temperature range per the Skyway Code?", "0°C to -15°C."], ["Surface visibility threshold below which VFR is not recommended?", "Less than 5 km."], ["Winds aloft above roughly what speed suggest bumpy conditions?", "About 35 kt."]], "quiz": [["Which chart forecasts weather below 10,000 ft over a 9-hour period?", ["F214", "F215", "GAMET", "SIGMET"], 1, "F215 is the low-level significant weather forecast."], ["In a TAF, SCT025 means:", ["3 to 4 oktas at 2,500 ft AAL", "3 to 4 oktas at 250 ft AMSL", "1 to 2 oktas at 2,500 ft AAL", "5 to 7 oktas at 2,500 ft AMSL"], 0, "SCT is 3 to 4 oktas and the height is above aerodrome level in hundreds of feet."], ["A cloud ceiling is:", ["Any cloud base", "Lowest cloud covering more than half the sky", "Cloud above 10,000 ft", "Cumulonimbus only"], 1, "BKN or OVC forms a ceiling. FEW and SCT do not."], ["A METAR reports visibility as 9999. This means:", ["999 m", "9,999 m exactly", "10 km or more", "Fog"], 2, "9999 is used for 10 km or more."], ["The Skyway Code says legal VFR visibility minima should be regarded as:", ["Targets", "Limits not targets", "Irrelevant", "Only for IFR"], 1, "Safe VFR flight normally ceases long before the legal minima."], ["Which product is a plain-text forecast issued four times a day for low-level GA?", ["GAMET", "F215", "SIGMET", "VOLMET"], 0, "GAMET replaced AIRMET and covers four UK regions."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.33-40", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 5, "topic": "Route planning and altitude selection (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "Plan a VFR route with a pilot's log (PLOG), error-check it, and back it up with a plotted chart. Choose a safety altitude by adding 500 or 1,000 ft to the Maximum Elevation Figure (MEF) and study at least 5 NM either side of track. Avoid whole-number cruising altitudes, plan descents at 3 NM per 1,000 ft, use Take 2 near controlled airspace and plan a transit about 10 minutes ahead. Built from the Skyway Code pp.41-45.", "sections": [["The PLOG", ["A pilot's log holds headings, distances and times for each leg, frequencies, planned and safety altitudes and the fuel plan", "Gross-error check all figures, for example confusing 030° with 300°, and keep winds aloft up to date", "Plot the route on a paper chart too: it is the backup if the moving map device fails", "Pick a radio frequency for each stage, consider UK FIS, and note hazards: aerodromes, danger areas, parachuting, glider sites and cable launches", "Many busy GA aerodromes have no ATZ. VFR traffic may bunch near visual reference points (VRPs) and beacons"]], ["Maximum Elevation Figure and safety altitude", ["The <b>MEF</b> is the large figure in each lat/long box on the VFR chart. It is the higher of the highest obstacle or the highest terrain plus <code>300 ft</code>", "Obstacles under 300 ft AGL may not be in the data, hence the 300 ft. Obstacles over 300 ft are captured with no extra margin", "Study the area at least <code>5 NM</code> either side of track", "No legal safety altitude exists for VFR but add <code>500 ft or 1,000 ft</code> over the highest obstacle or terrain", "Set safety altitudes below which you will not descend without reversing course or diverting", "If IFR-qualified, consider planning the flight as IFR from the outset"]], ["Choosing a cruising altitude", ["Avoid whole-number altitudes such as 3,000 ft. Pick random ones such as 4,200 ft or 5,400 ft", "Flying higher can give a better view, greater glide range, lower traffic density, less turbulence, better TAS and fuel burn, and better radio reception", "SERA VFR cruising levels apply above 3,000 ft AGL (starting at FL35) but are not mandatory in the UK. In some European states they are mandatory", "Descent planning rule: <code>3 NM for every 1,000 ft</code>, so about 30 NM from 10,000 ft. Ground speed x 5 gives the descent rate in ft/min (100 kt is about 500 ft/min, a 3° path)"]], ["Avoiding infringements", ["Study controlled airspace boundaries: vertical limits change over short distances", "Mark a route that clearly avoids CAS rather than navigating around edges ad hoc", "<b>Take 2</b>: keep <code>2 NM or 200 ft</code> from CAS, Danger and Restricted areas, ATZs and similar", "Request a UK FIS near CAS, or use a Frequency Monitoring Code", "Use the relevant aerodrome QNH, not the RPS. Do not use IFR waypoints for VFR navigation. Make user waypoints at recognisable features", "In local handling, set altitudes you must not exceed and include 'airspace' in HASELL checks"]], ["Planning a transit of CAS", ["Have a plan B. For CTRs check for preferred VFR routes in the aerodrome AIP entry (AD 2.22)", "Request about <b>10 minutes</b> before the boundary and calculate an ETA at the boundary", "Crossing overhead at right angles to the main runway can avoid aerodrome traffic", "Be ready for a different route or altitude. Overflying with vertical clearance is an effective avoidance"]]], "cards": [["What does a PLOG contain?", "Headings, distances, times, frequencies, planned and safety altitudes and the fuel plan."], ["What is the MEF?", "The higher of the highest obstacle or highest terrain plus 300 ft in a lat/long box."], ["Why the 300 ft in the MEF?", "Obstacles under 300 ft AGL may not be captured in the aeronautical data."], ["Suggested margin above the highest obstacle for a safety altitude?", "500 ft or 1,000 ft."], ["How wide should you study terrain either side of track?", "At least 5 NM."], ["Why choose non-whole-number cruising altitudes?", "To reduce the risk of conflict, such as 4,200 ft."], ["Descent rule of thumb?", "3 NM per 1,000 ft of height to lose."], ["Descent rate rule using ground speed?", "Ground speed x 5 gives ft/min, so 100 kt gives about 500 ft/min."], ["What is Take 2?", "Keep 2 NM or 200 ft from CAS and similar airspace."], ["How early to request a CAS transit?", "About 10 minutes before the boundary."], ["Is the RPS recommended for VFR near CAS?", "No, use the relevant aerodrome QNH."], ["Why avoid IFR waypoints in VFR navigation?", "They are often on CAS boundaries or approach routes, creating infringement risk."]], "quiz": [["The MEF in a lat/long box represents:", ["Average terrain height", "Higher of the highest obstacle or highest terrain plus 300 ft", "Safe cruising altitude", "Highest obstacle plus 1,000 ft"], 1, "The MEF is not a safe altitude on its own: add a margin."], ["You need to descend from 6,000 ft to 1,000 ft. Using the rule of thumb you need about:", ["5 NM", "15 NM", "25 NM", "50 NM"], 1, "3 NM per 1,000 ft x 5,000 ft of height to lose = 15 NM."], ["At 120 kt ground speed the rate of descent rule gives about:", ["300 ft/min", "600 ft/min", "900 ft/min", "1,200 ft/min"], 1, "Ground speed x 5 = 600 ft/min."], ["Take 2 means:", ["Two radio calls", "2 NM or 200 ft clear of airspace", "Two navigation aids", "Two crew"], 1, "It is the recommended margin from CAS and similar airspace."], ["Which safety altitude margin does the Skyway Code suggest?", ["100 ft", "500 ft or 1,000 ft", "2,000 ft", "None"], 1, "There is no legal requirement but 500 or 1,000 ft above the highest obstacle or terrain is appropriate."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.41-45", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 5, "topic": "Altimeter settings (Skyway Code)", "order": 35, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "The UK uses QNH (altitude above sea level), QFE (height above aerodrome), regional pressure setting (the lowest forecast pressure in a region) and standard 1013 hPa (flight levels). One hPa is about 27 ft. The transition altitude is normally 3,000 ft outside controlled airspace and 5,000 or 6,000 ft within and below it, and the transition level must be at least 1,000 ft above it. The RPS usually reads low. Built from the Skyway Code pp.90-91.", "sections": [["Four pressure settings", ["<b>QNH</b>: altitude above mean sea level. On the ground it reads aerodrome elevation", "<b>QFE</b>: height above aerodrome level. On the ground it reads zero", "<b>Regional pressure setting (RPS)</b>: the lowest forecast pressure in an altimeter setting region. The UK has <code>21</code> regions with geographic names such as Portland or Cotswold. It normally gives a lower reading than actual altitude", "<b>Standard 1013 hPa</b>: used above the transition altitude, giving flight levels and pressure altitude", "<code>1 hPa</code> is about <code>27 ft</code>"]], ["Transition altitude and level", ["TA is the altitude above which position is given as a flight level", "Outside CAS the UK transition altitude is normally <code>3,000 ft</code> AMSL. Within and below CAS it is normally <code>5,000 or 6,000 ft</code> (see ENR 1.7)", "It is not mandatory for VFR flights in the UK to change to FLs, but it is for IFR", "The lowest VFR flight level is normally FL35. The transition level is always at least <code>1,000 ft</code> above the TA", "Example: QNH 1000 and 3,000 ft altitude: set 1013 and the altimeter reads about <code>3,351 ft</code> (13 hPa x 27 ft)"]], ["Recommended procedure", ["Use the most relevant QNH for your route or area. Use the RPS only if no local QNH is available", "When transiting below or near CAS defined by altitude, use the QNH applicable to the airspace", "When near CAS defined by flight level, use 1013 hPa", "Larger aerodromes use QNH for take-off and landing"]]], "cards": [["What does QNH indicate?", "Altitude above mean sea level."], ["What does QFE indicate?", "Height above aerodrome level."], ["What is the standard pressure setting?", "1013 hPa."], ["How many feet per hPa?", "About 27 ft."], ["What is the RPS?", "The lowest forecast pressure in an altimeter setting region."], ["How many altimeter setting regions does the UK have?", "21."], ["Why is the RPS a risk near CAS?", "It normally under-reads, risking a vertical infringement."], ["UK transition altitude outside CAS?", "Normally 3,000 ft."], ["Transition altitude in or below CAS?", "Normally 5,000 or 6,000 ft."], ["Minimum gap between transition altitude and transition level?", "At least 1,000 ft."], ["What is the lowest VFR flight level?", "Normally FL35."], ["Is use of flight levels mandatory for VFR in the UK?", "No, it is required for IFR."]], "quiz": [["With QNH 1000 and the altimeter on 1013 at 3,000 ft altitude, the reading is about:", ["2,649 ft", "3,000 ft", "3,351 ft", "4,013 ft"], 2, "13 hPa x 27 ft = 351 ft, so 3,351 ft."], ["The regional pressure setting is:", ["The highest forecast pressure", "The lowest forecast pressure in the region", "An actual QNH", "1013 hPa"], 1, "Using RPS normally gives a lower altimeter reading than actual."], ["The transition level must be:", ["Equal to the TA", "At least 1,000 ft above the TA", "At least 500 ft above the TA", "FL100"], 1, "There must be a 1,000 ft minimum transition layer."], ["Near CAS defined as a flight level you should set:", ["QNH", "RPS", "1013 hPa", "QFE"], 2, "Use standard setting when the airspace is defined by FLs."], ["Which setting reads zero on the ground at an aerodrome?", ["QNH", "QFE", "RPS", "1013"], 1, "QFE reads height above the aerodrome."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.90-91", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 2, "topic": "Fuel, flight plans and aerodrome planning (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "Part 21 aircraft must carry certain reserves, and for non-Part 21 aircraft the Skyway Code advises not landing with less than 30 minutes of fuel for VFR. Gauges are not reliable so dip the tanks. A flight plan is required across borders, when under ATC, and when leaving the vicinity of an aerodrome at night. Check PPR, surface, usable length, obstacles and local procedures at unfamiliar aerodromes. Built from the Skyway Code pp.46-55.", "sections": [["Fuel management", ["Know fuel burn at each power setting and compare actual with planned during the flight", "Carry reserves proportionate to the flight. Non-Part 21 aircraft just need 'sufficient', but not less than <code>30 minutes</code> for VFR is recommended", "Pay attention to climb burn at high density altitude, cruise altitude and TAS, winds aloft and diversion options", "Fuel gauges are not reliable for planning: use a dipstick or equivalent. Never ignore a low gauge in flight", "A <b>fuel totaliser</b> measures fuel used, depends on the starting figure and would not show a leak", "Lean the mixture for cruise per the AFM. Several accidents came from fuel system misconfiguration, so understand tanks and selectors"]], ["Mass and balance reminders", ["Operating outside mass and balance limits is illegal. Use figures for your own airframe", "Avgas is about <code>0.72 kg/L</code> and Jet A-1 about <code>0.82 kg/L</code> (the leaflet uses about 0.8)", "CG = total moments / total mass (example 2,386.05 / 1,045 = 2.28 m)", "Check CG for take-off and landing as burning fuel can move it out of limits. Utility category may limit manoeuvres", "In most light aircraft you cannot fill the tanks and all the seats"]], ["Performance reminders", ["TORA, TODA, ASDA and LDA are in the AIP. Unlicensed strips often give only total length", "Do a calculation if mass, temperature, elevation or surface changes significantly. AFM data is for a new aircraft and proficient pilot", "Safety factors: dry grass x1.2 and x1.15, wet grass x1.3 and x1.35, soft ground or snow x1.25, then general x1.33 for take-off and x1.43 for landing", "Do not operate if factored distances exceed distances available", "Do not fly if the climb would be under <code>500 ft/min</code> in a typical single, or <code>150 ft/min</code> on a twin with one engine failed. Vx is slower than Vy and gives the better gradient"]], ["Flight plans", ["A flight plan is required for flights across international borders, flights under an ATC service (entering CAS or leaving a controlled aerodrome), and leaving the vicinity of an aerodrome at night", "'Booking out' fulfils the night abbreviated flight plan if an ATSU can receive it. Otherwise file a full plan", "Full plans go via AFTN. Abbreviated plans can be given by radio or phone and are not passed to other units", "Filing is recommended over water more than <code>10 NM</code> from the UK coast, to the Scottish Highlands and Islands, and over sparse areas"]], ["Unfamiliar aerodromes", ["The AIP covers licensed or certificated aerodromes only. Unlicensed information may be unverified", "Ask about PPR, local procedures, surface (long, wet or soft grass), usable length, obstacles, wind effects, noise abatement and other hazards", "Do the same for alternates. Considering alternates is often overlooked in GA"]]], "cards": [["Recommended minimum fuel at landing for VFR non-Part 21 aircraft?", "Not less than 30 minutes."], ["Best way to check fuel on board?", "Physically with a dipstick or equivalent."], ["What does a fuel totaliser measure?", "Fuel consumed by the engine, not tank contents."], ["Specific gravity of Avgas?", "About 0.72 kg/L."], ["Specific gravity of Jet A-1 in the Skyway Code?", "About 0.82 kg/L."], ["Climb rate below which not to fly in a single piston aircraft?", "500 ft/min."], ["Climb rate minimum on a twin with one engine failed?", "150 ft/min."], ["When must a flight plan be filed?", "International flights, under ATC service, and leaving the aerodrome vicinity at night."], ["What is an abbreviated flight plan?", "Information to an ATSU by radio or phone for part of a flight."], ["Over-water distance from the UK coast where a flight plan is recommended?", "More than 10 NM."], ["Does the AIP cover unlicensed aerodromes?", "No, only licensed or certificated ones."], ["Which speed gives the better climb gradient for obstacles?", "Vx."]], "quiz": [["What minimum fuel at landing is recommended for a VFR flight in non-Part 21 aircraft?", ["10 minutes", "30 minutes", "45 minutes", "60 minutes"], 1, "Part 21 aircraft have specific reserves, but for others 30 minutes is recommended."], ["A flight plan is required when:", ["Flying over any water", "Leaving the vicinity of an aerodrome at night", "Flying above 3,000 ft", "Flying solo"], 1, "Booking out can satisfy this if an ATSU receives it."], ["Which is the most reliable fuel quantity check?", ["Fuel gauge", "Totaliser", "Dipstick or equivalent", "Fuel flow estimate"], 2, "Gauges are not sufficiently reliable for planning."], ["A twin with one engine failed should not operate if it climbs below:", ["50 ft/min", "150 ft/min", "500 ft/min", "1,000 ft/min"], 1, "150 ft/min on a twin with an engine failed; 500 ft/min for singles."], ["Which is true of the general safety factors?", ["Applied before other factors", "Applied after other factors: x1.33 take-off, x1.43 landing", "Apply only to paved runways", "Apply only to twins"], 1, "Apply them after surface factors."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.46-55", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 0, "topic": "Rules of the Air essentials (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "The pilot in command has final authority, is responsible for compliance even when not at the controls, and must not fly impaired by psychoactive substances. Do not fly closer than 500 ft to any person, vessel, vehicle or structure, and over congested areas stay at least 1,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 600 m. In the air, head-on both turn right, converging the aircraft with the other on its right gives way, and the aircraft being overtaken has right of way. Built from the Skyway Code pp.57-63.", "sections": [["Which rules apply", ["UK Standardised Rules of the Air (UK SERA), the UK Rules of the Air Regulations 2015 and general permissions (see ORS4)", "In another state comply with both your state of registry's rules and the state you are in, whichever is more limiting. If they conflict the rules of the state you are in take precedence"]], ["Pilot in command", ["Has final authority over the aircraft and is always responsible for compliance, even if not touching the controls", "May depart from the rules only if absolutely necessary for safety", "Must be familiar with all available information and study weather and fuel and an alternative course of action for flights away from the aerodrome", "Must not act while under the influence of a psychoactive substance that impairs performance"]], ["Low flying and congested areas", ["Unless taking off or landing, do not fly closer than <code>500 ft</code> to any person, vessel, vehicle or structure", "Over congested areas or open-air assemblies stay at least <code>1,000 ft</code> above the highest obstacle within <code>600 m</code>, and high enough to land clear of people and property in an emergency", "UK exceptions include practice approaches, published low-level routes, Special VFR, hill soaring gliders and permitted events", "Do not take off or land in a congested area except at an aerodrome, or within 1,000 m of an open-air assembly of more than 1,000 people", "Aerobatics over congested areas are not allowed. Single-engine pilots should be able to glide clear of congested areas"]], ["Right of way in the air", ["Do not fly close to other aircraft so as to cause a collision hazard", "<b>Head-on</b>: both turn right", "<b>Converging</b>: the aircraft that has the other on its right gives way ('on the right, in the right')", "<b>Overtaking</b>: the aircraft being overtaken has right of way, and the overtaker passes to the right, keeping clear. Overtaking exists within 70° of the other's extended centreline. Sailplanes may pass either side", "Priority order when converging: balloons, gliders, airships, then aircraft towing, then powered aircraft last (powered aircraft give way to all others)", "Landing aircraft have priority. The lower aircraft has priority when two approach to land, but do not cut in front of one already on final. An aircraft compelled to land has priority", "Following a line feature on the right side is good practice but no longer mandatory"]], ["Right of way on the ground", ["Head-on: both stop and turn right", "Converging: the aircraft that has the other on its right gives way", "Overtaking: the overtaken aircraft has right of way and the overtaker may pass left or right", "Taxiing aircraft give way to aircraft taking off or about to take off", "Aircraft have priority over vehicles. Emergency vehicles have priority over all surface traffic"]]], "cards": [["Who has final authority over the aircraft?", "The pilot in command."], ["Closest approach allowed to any person, vessel, vehicle or structure (not taking off or landing)?", "500 ft."], ["Minimum height over congested areas?", "1,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 600 m."], ["Head-on in the air?", "Both turn right."], ["Converging aircraft: who gives way?", "The one that has the other on its right."], ["Overtaking aircraft pass on which side?", "The right (sailplanes may pass either side)."], ["Overtaking sector angle?", "Within 70° either side of the extended centreline."], ["Who gives way, a powered aircraft or a glider?", "The powered aircraft."], ["Who has priority when two aircraft approach to land?", "The lower one, but do not cut in front of one on final."], ["Head-on on the ground?", "Both stop and turn right."], ["Which aircraft taxiing give way to?", "Aircraft taking off or about to take off."], ["Which vehicles have priority over all surface traffic?", "Emergency vehicles assisting an aircraft in distress."]], "quiz": [["Two aeroplanes approach head-on. Each should:", ["Turn left", "Turn right", "Climb", "Descend"], 1, "Both alter course to the right."], ["You are converging with an aircraft on your right. You should:", ["Maintain course", "Give way", "Turn left", "Overtake"], 1, "The aircraft that has the other on its right gives way."], ["Over a congested area your minimum height is:", ["500 ft above ground", "1,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 600 m", "1,500 ft above ground", "2,000 ft AMSL"], 1, "It is also high enough to land clear in an emergency."], ["Who has priority among these in the air?", ["A glider over a powered aeroplane", "A powered aeroplane over a balloon", "An airship over a glider", "A powered aircraft over towing aircraft"], 0, "Powered heavier-than-air aircraft give way to gliders."], ["An aircraft is being overtaken. It has:", ["No right of way", "Right of way", "Right of way only on the ground", "To give way"], 1, "The aircraft being overtaken has right of way."], ["Where may you not take off or land in the UK without special permission?", ["Aerodrome", "Congested area (outside an aerodrome)", "Licensed strip", "ATZ"], 1, "Take-off and landing in a congested area need aerodrome procedures or CAA permission."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.57-63", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 0, "topic": "VFR, IFR and Special VFR (Skyway Code)", "order": 35, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "VMC minima depend on height and airspace class. At and above FL100 the minimum is 8 km visibility, 1,500 m horizontally and 1,000 ft vertically from cloud. Below FL100 it is 5 km with the same cloud distances, with UK exceptions for class D and class G at 140 kt IAS or less. VFR at night requires a cloud ceiling of at least 1,500 ft. Special VFR in a CTR needs 1,500 m visibility, clear of cloud, in sight of the surface and 140 kt IAS or less. Built from the Skyway Code pp.64-67.", "sections": [["VMC minima", ["At and above <code>FL100 / 10,000 ft</code> (all classes): <code>8 km</code> visibility, <code>1,500 m</code> horizontally and <code>1,000 ft</code> vertically from cloud", "Below FL100 and above 3,000 ft AMSL or 1,000 ft above terrain, whichever is higher: <code>5 km</code>, same cloud distances", "At and below 3,000 ft AMSL or 1,000 ft above terrain (classes A to E): 5 km, 1,500 m horizontal and 1,000 ft vertical. Class F and G: 5 km, clear of cloud and in sight of the surface", "<b>Class D exception (UK)</b>: by day at or below 3,000 ft AMSL, in sight of the surface and at 140 kt IAS or less you may fly clear of cloud, with 5 km visibility (1,500 m for helicopters)", "<b>Class G exception (UK)</b>: 5 km may reduce to 1,500 m by day in sight of the surface at 140 kt IAS or less", "VFR is not permitted in class A", "VMC minima exist to avoid other traffic (e.g. class E cloud rules keep VFR from IFR traffic)"]], ["Other VFR requirements", ["A VFR flight may not take off or land in a CTR, or enter the ATZ or circuit, if the reported cloud ceiling is under <code>1,500 ft</code> or ground visibility under <code>5 km</code>. Request Special VFR instead", "VFR flights may not operate above <code>FL195</code> unless authorised", "SERA VFR cruising levels are not mandatory in the UK"]], ["VFR at night (UK)", ["Same visibility and cloud rules as by day, except the 1,500 m class G reduction is not permitted", "Cloud ceiling must be at least <code>1,500 ft</code>", "Be in sight of the surface at or below 3,000 ft AMSL or 1,000 ft above terrain", "Never fly lower than 500 ft above ground or water, or 500 ft above the highest obstacle within 150 m. Over congested areas stay 1,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 600 m", "Submit a flight plan if leaving the vicinity of the aerodrome, and use an air traffic service if available", "En-route obstacles 150 m (490 ft) or more AGL are lit at night"]], ["Special VFR (SVFR)", ["Allows flight in a CTR below VMC minima without complying with IFR. Subject to ATC separation from IFR and other SVFR flights", "Minimum to accept a clearance: clear of cloud, in sight of the surface, flight visibility not less than <code>1,500 m</code> (<code>800 m</code> helicopters) and <code>140 kt</code> IAS or less", "For take-off or landing in the CTR, reported ground visibility must be at least 1,500 m (800 m helicopter) or a cloud ceiling of <code>600 ft</code>", "SVFR at night is permitted in the UK. Some aerodromes have local flying areas with standard SVFR arrangements"]], ["IFR", ["You must fly IFR if below VMC minima (unless in a CTR with SVFR clearance). Class A is IFR only", "IFR requires a valid instrument qualification", "IFR flights fly at least 1,000 ft (2,000 ft in mountainous areas) above the highest obstacle within 8 km (5 NM), except for take-off or landing", "Outside CAS, IFR cruising levels apply above 3,000 ft AMSL in the UK"]]], "cards": [["VMC visibility at and above FL100?", "8 km."], ["Horizontal and vertical cloud clearance in SERA VMC?", "1,500 m horizontally and 1,000 ft vertically."], ["Class D exception conditions?", "By day at or below 3,000 ft AMSL, in sight of the surface at 140 kt IAS or less."], ["Class G visibility reduction in the UK?", "5 km to 1,500 m by day, in sight of the surface at 140 kt IAS or less."], ["Is VFR permitted in class A?", "No."], ["Cloud ceiling needed to take off or land in a CTR under VFR?", "At least 1,500 ft, with 5 km ground visibility."], ["Upper limit for VFR flights unless authorised?", "FL195."], ["Cloud ceiling needed for VFR at night?", "At least 1,500 ft."], ["Minimum visibility for SVFR (aeroplane)?", "1,500 m, clear of cloud and in sight of the surface."], ["Maximum speed for SVFR?", "140 kt IAS."], ["Minimum reported ceiling to take off or land under SVFR?", "600 ft (or ground visibility 1,500 m)."], ["IFR terrain clearance outside mountainous areas?", "1,000 ft above highest obstacle within 8 km."]], "quiz": [["In Class E at FL100 or above the VFR visibility minimum is:", ["5 km", "8 km", "10 km", "1,500 m"], 1, "8 km at or above FL100."], ["In class G by day at 120 kt IAS and in sight of the surface, the UK visibility minimum can reduce to:", ["800 m", "1,500 m", "3 km", "5 km"], 1, "1,500 m by day, clear of cloud and in sight of the surface at 140 kt IAS or less."], ["The reported ceiling below which VFR cannot be used to land in a CTR is:", ["600 ft", "1,000 ft", "1,500 ft", "2,500 ft"], 2, "Below 1,500 ft ceiling or 5 km ground visibility, request Special VFR."], ["Special VFR minima for an aeroplane include:", ["800 m visibility", "1,500 m visibility and 140 kt IAS or less", "5 km visibility", "FL100"], 1, "Clear of cloud, in sight of the surface, 1,500 m and 140 kt or less."], ["Which statement is true about night VFR in the UK?", ["The 1,500 m class G reduction still applies", "Minima are the same as by day, except no 1,500 m reduction", "VFR is not allowed", "Ceiling of 600 ft is enough"], 1, "The reduced class G visibility is not permitted at night."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.64-67", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 0, "topic": "Airspace, ATZ, MATZ and transponders (Skyway Code)", "order": 40, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "An ATZ is a cylinder of 2 NM radius when the longest runway is 1,850 m or less, 2.5 NM otherwise, extending 2,000 ft above the aerodrome. A MATZ is not controlled airspace for civilians but contact is strongly recommended. Most UK GA controlled airspace is class D (CTRs and CTAs) with class A for airways and TMAs. Transponders should be on with altitude reporting (squawk 7000 VFR), and Mode S is needed in class A and C, above FL100 and in TMZs. Built from the Skyway Code pp.68-72 and 84-89.", "sections": [["Airspace structure", ["ICAO classes A to G exist but in UK airspace A, C, D, E and G are notified", "<b>Class A</b>: IFR only, clearance required, ATC service and separation for all flights", "Most controlled airspace GA meets is class D: <b>CTRs</b> (surface to about 2,500 ft AMSL or higher) and <b>CTAs</b> (base about 1,500 ft AMSL, overlying CTRs)", "<b>TMAs</b> cover several busy aerodromes and are normally class A (the Scottish TMA is an exception)", "<b>Airways</b> link structures and are normally class A. Some in Scotland are class E with a transponder mandatory zone, giving VFR access without clearance", "In class D there is no formal ATC separation of VFR from other traffic, only traffic information"]], ["ATZ and aerodromes without one", ["An ATZ is a cylinder around an aerodrome: <code>2 NM</code> radius if the longest runway is <code>1,850 m or less</code>, <code>2.5 NM</code> if longer, up to <code>2,000 ft</code> above the aerodrome", "You may cross an ATZ en route if you comply with Rule 11: with ATC permission is needed, with AFIS or AGCS get information", "Some aerodromes without an ATZ are shown with a circle and <b>T</b> (intensive training) or <b>U</b> (unusual activity such as aerobatics or formation)"]], ["MATZ", ["A MATZ is <b>not controlled airspace</b> for civilians but contains high-performance military aircraft, so contact the unit before entering", "It contains an ATZ: never enter that ATZ without a positive clearance", "Standard MATZ: 5 NM radius, 3,000 ft above airfield level, with two STUBS and LARS frequency", "Request a 'MATZ penetration service' about 10 minutes flying time ahead. If there is no reply after two attempts you may enter with caution", "MATZ are often inactive at weekends but check. Downwind and base legs of the radar training circuit can lie outside the MATZ"]], ["Requesting a transit", ["Start with the request: for example 'Solent Radar, G-DOME, request Basic Service and zone transit'", "On 'pass your message', give type, route, position, altitude and QNH. 'Standby' needs no acknowledgement", "Read back the squawk, altitude limits and service, and acknowledge future instructions with 'wilco'", "Remain clear until cleared. If no clearance is likely, alter course away positively"]], ["Transponder", ["Squawk <code>7000</code> VFR or <code>2000</code> IFR. Select Mode C (ALT) at all times", "Emergency codes: <code>7700</code> emergency, <code>7600</code> radio failure, <code>7500</code> unlawful interference, <code>0030</code> lost", "Frequency Monitoring Codes show you are listening on a frequency. Reset to conspicuity afterwards", "If you carry a serviceable transponder SERA requires it selected on with altitude. It can be turned off only on ATC direction, in airspace not needing carriage, or to save power", "<b>Mode S Elementary Surveillance</b> is needed in class A or C airspace, above FL100 and in TMZs"]]], "cards": [["ATZ radius for a runway of 1,850 m or less?", "2 NM."], ["ATZ radius for a runway over 1,850 m?", "2.5 NM."], ["ATZ height?", "Up to 2,000 ft above the aerodrome."], ["What do T and U mean on a chart circle?", "T is intensive training, U is unusual activity such as aerobatics or formation."], ["Is a MATZ controlled airspace for civil aircraft?", "No."], ["Standard MATZ dimensions?", "5 NM radius, 3,000 ft above airfield level."], ["Can you enter the ATZ within a MATZ without clearance?", "No."], ["Which class is a typical UK CTR?", "Class D."], ["Which class are airways and TMAs?", "Normally class A."], ["VFR conspicuity squawk?", "7000."], ["Squawk for lost?", "0030."], ["Where is Mode S ES required?", "Class A and C, above FL100, and TMZs."]], "quiz": [["The ATZ around an aerodrome with a 1,500 m runway has a radius of:", ["1 NM", "2 NM", "2.5 NM", "3 NM"], 1, "Runways of 1,850 m or less give a 2 NM ATZ."], ["A MATZ is:", ["Controlled airspace", "Not controlled airspace for civilians, but contact is strongly recommended", "Prohibited", "Class A"], 1, "Contact the unit before entering and never enter the ATZ without clearance."], ["Which class is most GA controlled airspace in the UK?", ["A", "B", "D", "F"], 2, "CTRs and CTAs are normally class D."], ["Which squawk shows a lost aircraft?", ["7500", "7600", "0030", "2000"], 2, "0030 is the UK lost code."], ["A circle with a T on the VFR chart means:", ["Transit allowed", "Intensive training", "Tower", "TMZ"], 1, "T denotes intensive use for training flights."], ["Mode S Elementary Surveillance is required:", ["In class G", "In class A or C airspace, above FL100 and in TMZs", "Only at night", "Only IFR"], 1, "Those are the circumstances listed by the Skyway Code."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.68-72, 84-89", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 6, "topic": "Aerodrome operations and circuits (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "At aerodromes you observe other traffic, conform with the circuit, turn left unless told otherwise, and land and take off into wind. You may not land on an occupied runway without ATC authority. During notified watch hours you may not operate in an ATZ without permission (ATC) or information (AFIS/AGCS), and you must report position and height entering and leaving. Joining aircraft give way to traffic in the circuit. Built from the Skyway Code pp.96-99.", "sections": [["Operating rules (SERA.3225)", ["Observe other traffic to avoid collision, conform with or avoid the circuit pattern", "All turns to the <b>left</b> when approaching to land or take off unless otherwise indicated", "Land and take off <b>into wind</b> unless safety dictates otherwise", "On landing, leave clear to your left any other aircraft landing or about to take off, and move clear of the landing area as soon as possible", "When taking up position for take-off leave clear on your left any aircraft that has taken off or is about to"]], ["ATZ rules (Rule 11 and 12)", ["Rule 10: do not land on a runway occupied by another aircraft unless authorised by ATC", "Rule 11: during notified hours of ATC, AFIS or AGCS, do not operate within an ATZ unless you have permission (ATC) or have obtained information (AFIS or AGCS)", "Keep a continuous watch on the frequency or for visual signals and transmit position and height on entering or leaving the ATZ", "Rule 12: do not taxi on the manoeuvring area of an uncontrolled aerodrome without the permission of the person in charge or the AFIS unit"]], ["The circuit", ["Legs: crosswind, downwind, base, final, with the liveside and deadside", "Standard radio calls at downwind and final. Also announce base or late downwind if it helps situational awareness", "Joining aircraft give way to traffic already established", "Always make position calls even if you know of no other traffic, with blind calls if no AGCS or AFIS. Non-radio aircraft are a possibility", "If in doubt on local procedure, ask by phone or radio. AFIS and AGCS will not give instructions to airborne aircraft"]], ["Departure procedures", ["Obtain runway in use, circuit direction and QNH or QFE before taxiing. Larger aerodromes provide ATIS: quote the information letter", "At a controlled aerodrome request permission to taxi. At an AGCS aerodrome no taxi clearance is needed but you must have the permission of the person in charge. At an AFIS aerodrome request taxi instructions", "Complete power and pre-take-off checks at the holding point before saying 'ready for departure'", "At an AFIS aerodrome the service reverts to information only: the pilot ensures it is safe to enter the runway", "At an ATC aerodrome you must not enter the runway without clearance (take-off clearance or 'line up and wait')", "Stop at an angle at the holding point for a good view of the approach, particularly in a high-wing aircraft", "Report leaving the ATZ. At large aerodromes, moving-map aerodrome charts are helpful. <b>Hot spots</b> are circled in red on charts"]]], "cards": [["Which way do you turn in the circuit unless told otherwise?", "Left."], ["Which direction do you land and take off?", "Into wind unless safety dictates otherwise."], ["What does Rule 10 say about runways?", "Do not land on an occupied runway unless authorised by ATC."], ["What does Rule 11 apply to?", "Operating within an ATZ during notified hours of watch."], ["What does Rule 12 apply to?", "Taxiing on the manoeuvring area of an uncontrolled aerodrome."], ["Do you need a taxi clearance at an AGCS aerodrome?", "No, but you need the permission of the person in charge."], ["What do you request at an AFIS aerodrome before taxiing?", "Taxi instructions."], ["Who must you give way to when joining the circuit?", "Traffic already established."], ["What must be transmitted on entering or leaving an ATZ?", "Aircraft position and height."], ["What are hot spots on aerodrome charts?", "Holding points vulnerable to runway incursion, circled in red."], ["Which clearance allows runway entry at an ATC aerodrome?", "Take-off clearance or 'line up and wait'."], ["What must you call after departing the ATZ?", "Report leaving the ATZ."]], "quiz": [["Unless told otherwise, circuit turns are:", ["Right", "Left", "Either", "Depends on wind"], 1, "All turns left when approaching to land or take off unless indicated."], ["At an AFIS aerodrome, when you report 'ready for departure':", ["You receive a take-off clearance", "The service reverts to information only and you ensure it is safe", "ATC separates you", "You must hold"], 1, "AFIS gives information, not clearances, once you are at the holding point."], ["Rule 11 requires, in an ATZ with an AGCS, that you:", ["Get permission", "Obtain appropriate information", "Hold off", "Squawk 7700"], 1, "For AFIS or AGCS you get information, with ATC you get permission."], ["Joining traffic must:", ["Have priority", "Give way to traffic already in the circuit", "Join on final", "Squawk ident"], 1, "Joining aircraft give way to established traffic."], ["Before taxiing at an aerodrome with ATIS, you should quote:", ["The QFE", "The information letter on first contact", "The squawk", "Fuel on board"], 1, "Give the ATIS letter on first contact with ATC."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.96-99", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 6, "topic": "Emergencies (Skyway Code)", "order": 35, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "Know your aircraft's checklists, fly the aircraft, assess, then declare an emergency in good time (on 121.5 MHz if not talking to anyone). The call format follows MAYDAY or PAN PAN three times, station, callsign, type, nature, intentions, position, altitude, heading and other information. D&D at London Area Control Centre can give fast position fixes. After engine failure land ahead after take-off and use a constant-aspect approach to a field. Built from the Skyway Code pp.136-141.", "sections": [["Key principles", ["Know the AFM checklists for time-critical emergencies by heart", "<b>Fly the aircraft</b>: accidents often happen when a non-lethal problem distracts from flying. Keep a safe flying speed", "<b>Assess</b> before acting. Do not jump to conclusions", "<b>Declare</b> an emergency in good time. It is easier to cancel than to start too late. If not with an ATSU use <code>121.5 MHz</code>", "Select <code>7700</code>. 'Mayday out' means no reply needed"]], ["Mayday and Pan call format", ["Distress: 'Mayday' three times. Urgency: 'Pan Pan' three times", "Then: station addressed, callsign, aircraft type, nature of emergency, intentions, position, altitude, heading, other information (persons on board, pilot qualifications)", "There is no ICAO requirement to include pilot qualifications but doing so helps UK controllers"]], ["Distress and Diversion Cell (D&D)", ["The emergency centre at the London Area Control Centre, available <b>24 hours</b> to pilots in UK airspace in distress or with difficulties such as being unsure of position", "Can give accurate position fixes by direction finding. Instant fixes are available in the London FIR at or above <code>3,000 ft</code> AMSL over land east of Wales and south of Manchester, and in the Scottish FIR at or above 8,500 ft", "Also takes practice calls ('Practice PAN' three times) and training fixes if no real emergency is in progress"]], ["Lost, comms and electrical failure", ["<b>Lost</b>: squawk <code>0030</code>, ask a radar ATSU or call D&D on 121.5, orbit a landmark and do not fly aimlessly", "<b>Comms loss</b>: check volume, squelch, frequency and audio panel first, then set <code>7600</code>, stay in VMC and consider a quiet aerodrome outside CAS. Overhead observe the signal square and light signals", "<b>Electrical failure</b>: alternator failure is common and the battery may give about 20 minutes. Reduce load, expect to lose the radio, know which systems are electric (flaps)"]], ["Engine failure and field selection", ["Know your best glide speed and procedure", "Shortly after take-off <b>land ahead</b>, rather than turning back", "With height check common causes: fuel selection and carb icing", "With a partial failure decide positively between a field and an aerodrome", "Field choice: wind (into wind), size (bigger), surroundings (power lines), shape (square), surface (grass is better than ploughed fields or crops) and slope (avoid)", "<b>Constant aspect</b> approach: enter the pattern, assess the visual angle at about 45°, keep that angle constant by tightening or widening the turn, aim further in if using full flap or gear, and side slip or S-turn if high"]], ["Fire and ditching", ["Fire on the ground: usually when starting. Cut mixture and fuel, keep cranking with the throttle open to draw fire back, otherwise leave upwind", "Fire in the air: engine or electrical. Shut down and force land. Do not reset a circuit breaker more than once", "Ditching: wear lifejackets (belt over the jacket) and carry a liferaft for significant water crossings. In winter you are unlikely to survive more than an hour immersed unless in a survival suit"]]], "cards": [["What frequency do you use if not talking to an ATSU in an emergency?", "121.5 MHz."], ["What squawk shows an emergency?", "7700."], ["What does 'Mayday out' mean?", "No further reply is needed."], ["What is D&D?", "The Distress and Diversion Cell at London Area Control Centre."], ["When is D&D available?", "24 hours a day."], ["Squawk if lost?", "0030."], ["Squawk for radio failure?", "7600."], ["How long might a battery last after alternator failure?", "Perhaps 20 minutes."], ["What to do after engine failure just after take-off?", "Land ahead rather than turn back."], ["Best field surface?", "Grass, better than ploughed fields or crops."], ["What is a constant-aspect approach?", "Keep the visual angle to the landing spot constant by adjusting the turn."], ["Maximum circuit breaker resets for a suspected fire risk?", "Never reset more than once."], ["Immersion survival time in winter without a suit?", "Unlikely more than an hour."]], "quiz": [["If not talking to a ATSU and you have an emergency, call on:", ["118.0", "121.5", "123.45", "135.48"], 1, "121.5 MHz is the emergency frequency monitored by D&D."], ["In the call order, after the station you give:", ["Intentions", "Callsign, then aircraft type", "Position", "Fuel"], 1, "Station, callsign, type, nature, intentions, position, altitude, heading, other information."], ["After engine failure just after take-off you should:", ["Turn back", "Land ahead", "Climb", "Stretch the glide"], 1, "Landing ahead is safer than attempting a turn back."], ["After alternator failure the battery may give about:", ["2 minutes", "20 minutes", "2 hours", "Unlimited power"], 1, "Reduce loads to extend it."], ["Radio failure: before concluding you have a failure you should check:", ["Nothing", "Volume, squelch, frequency and audio selector panel", "Only the squawk", "Fuel"], 1, "Many apparent failures are caused by incorrect settings."], ["Which is a good forced-landing field surface?", ["Ploughed field", "Crops", "Grass", "Steep slope"], 2, "Grass is one of the better surfaces."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.136-141", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}, {"s": 3, "topic": "Decision making and threat and error management (Skyway Code)", "order": 30, "conf": "weak", "status": "draft", "summary": "Good decisions start before flight. Use PAVE (Pilot, Aircraft, EnVironment, External pressures) and I AM SAFE, ask 'what if?', and avoid time pressure. Threat and error management (TEM) has three elements: threats, errors and undesired aircraft states, and aims to manage them rather than eliminate them. Threats are anticipated, unanticipated or latent. Errors are handling, procedural or communication. Distraction and overload are common causes. Built from the Skyway Code pp.122-125.", "sections": [["Pre-flight decisions", ["Use a pre-flight checklist and consider the <b>cumulative</b> effect of risk factors. The decision to start the flight is often the most critical", "<b>PAVE</b>: <b>P</b>ilot (recency, fitness, I AM SAFE), <b>A</b>ircraft (airworthiness, limits), En<b>V</b>ironment (weather, aerodrome, terrain), <b>E</b>xternal pressures (time, delays, passengers)", "Gather NOTAMs and weather, think about how it will evolve and identify diversion aerodromes, not just a return to the start", "Always ask 'what if?' and take positive decisions: do not wait 'hoping it will be OK'", "Match the flight to your recent experience. Arrive in good time and remove distractions. Check the weather and NOTAMs the evening before for an early flight, then again on the day"]], ["Stress and in-flight decisions", ["If stressed, fatigued or unsure of fitness, defer the flight or fly with an instructor", "Major emotional events such as bereavement can reduce focus below a safe level. Chronic stress signs: poor sleep, appetite, indigestion, irritability, poor concentration", "Think a few minutes ahead of the aircraft and use quiet periods for routine checks", "Monitor the wider weather: if you are being forced lower, a new plan is needed", "In most non-normal situations pause and assess before reacting, unless the aircraft is in immediate danger. Emergencies such as engine failure need rehearsed procedures"]], ["Threat and error management", ["TEM is 'defensive flying'. It aims to manage threats and errors, not to eliminate them", "Three elements: <b>threats</b>, <b>errors</b> and <b>undesired aircraft states</b> (an unsafe attitude, configuration or location)", "<b>Threats</b> are outside the pilot's direct influence: <b>anticipated</b> (poor forecast, dense VFR traffic, short runway, known fault), <b>unanticipated</b> and <b>latent</b> (time pressure, fatigue, insufficient skill, poor safety attitude)", "<b>Errors</b> are actions or inactions that reduce safety: <b>aircraft handling</b>, <b>procedural</b> or <b>communication</b>", "Handling errors reflect low recency. Procedural and communication errors often come from poor checklist discipline or RT proficiency", "A common cause of all error types is <b>distraction and overload</b>. Mitigate with planning, anticipation, task prioritisation and removing distractions"]]], "cards": [["What does PAVE stand for?", "Pilot, Aircraft, EnVironment, External pressures."], ["What does TEM stand for?", "Threat and error management."], ["What are the three elements of TEM?", "Threats, errors and undesired aircraft states."], ["What is an undesired aircraft state?", "An unsafe attitude, configuration or location."], ["Name the three categories of threat.", "Anticipated, unanticipated and latent."], ["Give an example of a latent threat.", "Time pressure, fatigue, insufficient skill or poor safety attitude."], ["Name three types of error.", "Aircraft handling, procedural and communication."], ["What is a common cause of all error types?", "Distraction and mental overload."], ["What should you avoid when deciding to fly?", "Waiting 'to see what happens' or 'hoping it will be OK'."], ["What does TEM aim to do?", "Manage threats and errors, not eliminate them."], ["What should you do in most non-normal situations?", "Pause and assess before reacting, unless in immediate danger."], ["What should you do if stressed or fatigued?", "Defer the flight or fly with an instructor."]], "quiz": [["In PAVE, the E for External pressures includes:", ["Engine", "Time pressure and passengers", "Weather", "Fuel"], 1, "Time pressure, delays and passengers are external pressures."], ["A forecast of poor weather is which kind of threat?", ["Latent", "Anticipated", "Unanticipated", "Error"], 1, "It is known in advance."], ["Fatigue and time pressure are examples of:", ["Unanticipated threats", "Latent threats", "Handling errors", "Undesired aircraft states"], 1, "Latent threats are background factors."], ["A procedural error often comes from:", ["Insufficient checklist discipline", "Excess altitude", "Engine icing", "Radar"], 0, "Procedural and communication errors link to checklist and RT discipline."], ["TEM is best described as:", ["Eliminating all risk", "Defensive flying that manages threats and errors", "A navigation method", "An emergency drill"], 1, "TEM aims to manage them more effectively."]], "src": [["The Skyway Code (CAP 1535 v4, Nov 2023), pp.122-125", "https://www.caa.co.uk/our-work/publications/documents/content/cap1535s/"]]}]);
